// expect: 7
// SYNTAX §«Imports» — a module's IDENTITY is the module it resolves to,
// never the spelling that reached it. The prelude imports lists for every
// program, so importing it here draws a SECOND edge to a module already in
// the DAG — and drawing an edge that exists is a no-op, because that is what
// an edge is: the walk keys its visited set by the PATH a spelling resolved
// to, so the module is collected once and no declaration in it collides with
// itself (58 diagnostics on this four-line program the day the set keyed the
// spelling, while the identical source through the blob link was silent, the
// concatenation never resolving an import at all — the divergence this
// battery's manifest leg exists to catch).
//
// Two spellings of ONE file from one program is a user-folder shape — sub/c
// from the entry and c from its sibling in sub/ — and the frontier's
// user-link leg holds it (tests/frontier/user-link/nest). A lib/-prefixed
// spelling is not a module name a program can write: a user module resolves
// against its own folder and the library roots, never the repository's
// layout (SYNTAX §«Imports», the layer law).
import lists

fn main() = len([1, 2, 3]) + 4
